# HD Documentation

HD is a body-only HTML documentation format authored in a dedicated VS Code WYSIWYG editor. It targets the documentation use cases Markdown can't cleanly express — multi-paragraph table cells, `colspan`/`rowspan`, inline styles, inline SVG — without dragging in full-page HTML concerns like `<head>`, scripts, and stylesheets.

## What's in this folder

-   [Getting Started](getting-started.hd) — install the extension and create your first document.
    
-   [Use Cases](use-cases.hd) — common ways teams use HD: user-facing documentation, internal docs, AI-collaborated notes.
    
-   [VS Code Extension](vscode-extension.hd) — what the extension is, why HD authoring lives inside VS Code, what it registers.
    
-   [Editor Features](editor.hd) — toolbar, table manipulation, paste behavior, clipboard interop.
    
-   [Format Specification](format-spec.hd) — file structure, frontmatter, allowed elements and attributes, Markdown round-trip behavior.
    
-   [Format Comparison](format-compare.hd) — how HD stacks up against Markdown, HTML, and Nextra docs.
    
-   [Assets](assets.hd) — how images and binary assets are stored and managed.
    
-   [Sync to External Platforms](sync.hd) — publishing `.hd` source to Nextra, Docusaurus, GitBook, and friends via the `/hd:sync` slash command.
    

## Quick characterization

-   **Body-only HTML.** Files contain only the content that would sit inside `<body>`. No `<html>`, `<head>`, `<script>`, or `<style>` tags.
    
-   **Optional YAML frontmatter.** Bare `---` fence at the top, Markdown-style. No HTML comment wrapper.
    
-   **Per-document base62 identity.** The editor injects a stable `id:` on first open. Asset folders are keyed by that id, so renames and moves don't break references.
    
-   **Format version field.** The current version is `1`. Documents without a `version:` field are interpreted as the latest version.
    
-   **Markdown round-trip via clipboard.** Paste Markdown into the editor and it converts; "Copy as Markdown" emits Markdown for round-trippable elements and raw HTML for everything else.
